ntdll: Bound the free list scan in find_free_block().

find_free_block() starts at the free list for the requested size class
and walks it until a block is large enough. That first list holds blocks
from somewhat smaller than the request upwards; every block in a later
list fits. When fragmentation leaves many too-small blocks there, every
allocation of that size walks all of them, and the cost grows with the
square of their number.

Affinity hit this opening a 100 MB SVG of 1.87 million rectangles in a
session that had already been used for other documents: its UI thread
spent 96% of its time in find_free_block(), called from group_allocate()
(the low-fragmentation heap taking a new group from the backend heap),
and the application never recovered.

Check at most 32 blocks of the first list, then take the first block of
the next non-empty one. Blocks of the first list that fit are still
found and reused.

A test that pins N free 36 KiB blocks between used blocks and then
allocates N slightly larger ones:

                        before      after
  N = 5000              638 ms      19 ms
  N = 20000           13824 ms      70 ms
  N fitting allocs       19 ms      19 ms

The kernel32 heap conformance tests give the same result before and
after (278617 tests, 0 failures).

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/heap.c b/dlls/ntdll/heap.c
index feee54e..d6102e7 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/heap.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/heap.c
@@ -1106,21 +1106,40 @@ static SUBHEAP *create_subheap( struct heap *heap, DWORD flags, SIZE_T total_siz
 }
 
 
+/* how many blocks of the first free list find_free_block() checks before moving on to the next list */
+#define FREE_LIST_SCAN_MAX 32
+
 static struct block *find_free_block( struct heap *heap, ULONG flags, SIZE_T block_size )
 {
-    struct list *ptr = &find_free_list( heap, block_size, FALSE )->entry;
+    struct entry *list = find_free_list( heap, block_size, FALSE );
+    struct list *ptr = &list->entry;
+    unsigned int scanned = 0;
+    BOOL first_list = TRUE;
     struct entry *entry;
     struct block *block;
     SIZE_T total_size;
     SUBHEAP *subheap;
 
-    /* Find a suitable free list, and in it find a block large enough */
+    /* Find a suitable free list, and in it find a block large enough. The first list holds blocks
+     * from somewhat smaller than block_size up, and every block in the later lists fits. After a
+     * heavily fragmented heap left many too-small blocks in the first list, scanning all of them on
+     * every allocation made it quadratic, so give up on it after a few and take the next list's. */
 
     while ((ptr = list_next( &heap->free_lists[0].entry, ptr )))
     {
         entry = LIST_ENTRY( ptr, struct entry, entry );
         block = &entry->block;
-        if (block_get_flags( block ) == BLOCK_FLAG_FREE_LINK) continue;
+        if (block_get_flags( block ) == BLOCK_FLAG_FREE_LINK)
+        {
+            first_list = FALSE;
+            continue;
+        }
+        if (first_list && ++scanned > FREE_LIST_SCAN_MAX && list + 1 < heap->free_lists + FREE_LIST_COUNT)
+        {
+            first_list = FALSE;
+            ptr = &list[1].entry;
+            continue;
+        }
         if (block_get_size( block ) >= block_size)
         {
             if (!subheap_commit( heap, block_get_subheap( heap, block ), block, block_size )) return NULL;
